// Kiddo School — NATURE CLUB · Animals:
// /nature-club/animals/learn/ lessons. One simple kid spotter guide per
// animal group, in the set's walk order (birds of the world → by the water →
// little crawlies → fur families → wild homes). The original flashcard sits
// LEFT with the drawing canvas; this guide sits RIGHT, written so a KID can
// follow it: what the group is, who belongs, how to tell look-alikes apart,
// and one way to spot or play the group at home. Written fresh — the library
// pages in data-nature-club-animals.mjs answer WHAT the group is and WHAT
// the animals do; no text is shared between the two. The US/UK/CA pairs
// (reindeer/caribou, moose/elk, shrimp/prawn, buzzard/vulture) are named in
// both flavors where the animals carry them. The age story is honest: this
// is the deeper deck — most lessons name themselves for ages 4–5, with the
// friendliest groups riding Ages 3+.
export const lpContent={
 'birds-of-prey':{
  title:'The Birds of Prey Lesson',one:'The hunters of the sky.',
  seoTitle:'Birds of Prey Lesson for Kids — Eagle, Hawk, Falcon and Osprey, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: birds of prey — eagle, hawk, falcon and osprey. How raptors hunt, how to tell a falcon from a hawk, and the hawk-eyes spotting game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the sky&rsquo;s hunters and how to tell them apart, one silhouette at a time.',
  what:'Birds of prey (raptors) are the hunting birds: hooked beaks, gripping talons and famous eyesight — they spot their dinner from higher than a rooftop.',
  mean:'Sharp tools for sharp work: the beak tears, the feet grip, and the eyes do the finding — every raptor part is built for the hunt.',
  know:'Eagles are the giants; hawks circle in wide lazy loops; falcons are streamlined and dive at over 300 km/h; ospreys hover over water and catch fish feet-first.',
  why:'Raptors live across the US, UK and Canada — the red-tailed hawk on a lamppost is a wild raptor in plain sight, and this card is its ID guide.',
  dev:'Silhouette recognition, patient looking, and food-chain respect: hunters keep the wild healthy.',
  vocab:'raptor, bird of prey, eagle, hawk, falcon, osprey, talons, hover, dive, silhouette',
  teach:'1) The raptor test: hooked beak + gripping feet + hunter&rsquo;s eyes — all three, always.\n2) The eagle: the heavyweight — a nest bigger than a bathtub, used for years.\n3) The hawk: the circler — watch it ride warm air in slow loops without flapping, scanning the ground.\n4) The falcon: the diver — the fastest animal alive in its stoop; the peregrine is a city balcony hunter in many towns.\n5) The osprey: the fisher — hovers, then crashes feet-first; talons front-facing for gripping slippery dinner.',
  say:'"Birds of prey hunt with eyes, beaks and feet! The eagle is the giant, the hawk rides circles on the warm air, the falcon dives faster than anything alive, and the osprey plucks fish from the water — splash!"',
  notice:'On the card, the eagle perches biggest, the hawk shows the classic broad wings, the falcon stands sleek and pointed, and the osprey grips with its hunter feet. Which shape looks built for speed?',
  ask:'"What do raptors hunt with?" (eyes to find, feet to grip, beak to eat) "Which bird dives the fastest?" (the falcon) "Which one catches fish?" (the osprey)',
  childSay:'Your child might circle the yard like a hawk on a scanning patrol, fist-dive from the couch like a falcon, or classify every crow in the sky before correcting themselves: "no — wrong beak."',
  real:'Hawk-watching is free: lampposts and fence posts across the US, UK and Canada host red-tailed hawks daily. Raptor centers take injured hunters and fly them at demonstrations.',
  play:'Hawk-eyes minute: from a window, spot three tiny things a hawk could see — a coin, a leaf, a crack. Then the falcon dive: one fast, silent drop from standing to a crouch, timed by the crowd.',
  check:'Ask: "What three things does every raptor have?" (hooked beak, gripping talons, sharp eyes) "How does the osprey catch fish?" (hovers, then dives feet-first) "What is a falcon famous for?" (the fastest dive on Earth)'},
 'garden-birds':{
  title:'The Garden Birds Lesson',one:'The neighbors at the feeder.',
  seoTitle:'Garden Birds Lesson for Kids — Robin, Woodpecker, Blue Jay and Cardinal, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 3+: garden birds — robin, woodpecker, blue jay and cardinal. How to start a bird count at your own window, and the drumming sound of the woodpecker.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the backyard regulars, one feeder-visit at a time.',
  what:'Garden birds are the birds that live where people do — the robin, woodpecker, blue jay and cardinal are the backyard feeder&rsquo;s most famous regulars.',
  mean:'Wild does not mean far away: a whole neighborhood of wild birds operates at every window, and learning four names opens it.',
  know:'Robins hop close and cock their heads to listen for worms; woodpeckers drum up to twenty beats a second on hollow trees; blue jays bury acorns like squirrels and mimic hawk calls; cardinals are the all-red flag of US yards.',
  why:'A feeder or a scattered handful of seed turns this card into a daily live show — bird names are the child&rsquo;s first citizen-science.',
  dev:'Sound identification (drumming, squawk, song), counting and tallying, and the patience of quiet watching.',
  vocab:'garden bird, robin, woodpecker, drum, blue jay, cardinal, feeder, seed, tally, perch',
  teach:'1) Set the stage: a little seed on a sill or feeder, ten quiet minutes — the neighbors arrive.\n2) The robin: hops, stops, head-cocked — listening for worms under the grass.\n3) The woodpecker: braces its stiff tail like a kickstand and drums — twenty taps a second, and a built-in helmet absorbs the knock.\n4) The blue jay: the loud one in blue — it buries acorns (planting oaks like the squirrel) and can imitate a hawk to clear the feeder.\n5) The cardinal: the red flag — the male is all scarlet with a crest; in winter snow, he is the brightest thing in the yard.',
  say:'"Garden birds are the neighbors! The robin listens for worms, the woodpecker drums on the tree — tok-tok-tok — the blue jay squawks the news, and the cardinal is the reddest bird in the yard. Who visits your window?"',
  notice:'On the card, the robin perches forward, the woodpecker grips its tree trunk, the blue jay shows its crest and long tail, and the cardinal stands tall in full red. Which beak looks built for drilling wood?',
  ask:'"Which garden bird drums on trees?" (the woodpecker) "Which one is all red?" (the cardinal) "What does the blue jay bury like a squirrel?" (acorns)',
  childSay:'Your child might keep a sticker tally on the window for a week, drum pencil-rhythms on every hollow thing "like a woodpecker", or assign a name to the yard&rsquo;s regular cardinal.',
  real:'The Great Backyard Bird Count every February invites families to count birds for science for fifteen minutes — this card is the training. Woodpecker drumming carries farthest on still mornings.',
  play:'Counting feeder: tally stickers — one per bird visit, name each species if you can. Then the woodpecker echo: one person drums a rhythm on the table, the other repeats it exactly.',
  check:'Ask: "How does the robin find worms?" (listens with a head-cock) "What does the woodpecker&rsquo;s drumming say?" ("this tree is mine") "Which bird is the red one?" (the cardinal)'},
 'desert-birds':{
  title:'The Desert Birds Lesson',one:'Birds where rain is rare.',
  seoTitle:'Desert Birds Lesson for Kids — Roadrunner, Desert Owl, Vulture and Sandgrouse, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: desert birds — roadrunner, desert owl, vulture and sandgrouse. The buzzard word mix-up (US vs UK), the water-carrying feathers and the roadrunner relay.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the birds that own the hot sand, one trick at a time.',
  what:'Desert birds survive where water hides: the roadrunner sprints instead of flying, the desert owl sleeps out the heat, the vulture soars on hot air, and the sandgrouse carries water home in its feathers.',
  mean:'Every desert animal is a walking invention — the birds invent too: running legs, feather bottles, and riding the warm wind without a wingbeat.',
  know:'Roadrunners can sprint at 30 km/h and rarely fly far; desert owls hunt at night when the sand cools; vultures ride thermals for hours; sandgrouse fathers soak their belly feathers and fly up to 30 km to water the chicks.',
  mean2:'',
  why:'The buzzard mix-up makes this card the deck&rsquo;s word-history lesson: one word, two birds, two sides of an ocean.',
  dev:'Word-geography (buzzard means different birds in the US and UK), thermal-soaring physics in kid terms (warm air is an elevator), and water-respect.',
  vocab:'desert, roadrunner, sprint, desert owl, vulture, buzzard, sandgrouse, thermal, soar, heat',
  teach:'1) The roadrunner: the sprinter — it runs down lizards and even rattlesnakes; flying is for emergencies only. Beep beep!\n2) The desert owl: the night nurse — sleeps the scorching day away in a shady hole, hunts when the sand cools.\n3) The vulture: the cleaner — rides the rising warm air (thermals) in wide circles for hours without flapping, tidying the desert of what nature leaves behind.\n4) The word trap: in North America people call vultures "buzzards", but in Britain a buzzard is a HAWK — same word, two birds, and now you know both.\n5) The sandgrouse: the feather bottle — the father soaks his belly feathers at the water hole and flies the water home for the chicks to drink.',
  say:'"Desert birds have the best tricks! The roadrunner sprints — beep beep! — the owl sleeps out the heat, the vulture rides the warm wind round and round, and the sandgrouse carries water home in its feathers. And the buzzard? Two different birds on two sides of the ocean!"',
  notice:'On the card, the roadrunner runs with its long tail out, the desert owl squints in the shade, the vulture spreads the widest wings, and the sandgrouse stands plump and round. Which two never flap much?',
  ask:'"How does the roadrunner mostly travel?" (sprinting — running is its flying) "What does the vulture ride?" (warm rising air — thermals) "What did the sandgrouse father carry?" (water in soaked belly feathers)',
  childSay:'Your child might sprint every hallway "roadrunner style", ride imaginary thermals with statue arms, or correct a grandparent&rsquo;s buzzard with the two-countries lesson.',
  real:'Vultures soaring on thermals are visible from any desert highway in the US Southwest — the wobble-and-circle is unmistakable. Real roadrunners live across the region and do sprint across roads.',
  play:'Roadrunner relay: sprint the yard on foot, wings optional. Then the thermal float: circle the room with still arms, no flapping, slowest laps win. Finish with the feather-bottle walk: carry a cup of water across the room in a chest pocket.',
  check:'Ask: "What does a buzzard mean in Britain?" (a hawk) "What does it mean in North America?" (a vulture) "How does the sandgrouse bring water?" (soaked belly feathers)'},
 'australian-birds':{
  title:'The Australian Birds Lesson',one:'The strangest birds on Earth.',
  seoTitle:'Australian Birds Lesson for Kids — Emu, Kookaburra, Cassowary and Lyrebird, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: Australian birds — the giant emu, the laughing kookaburra, the helmeted cassowary and the copycat lyrebird. Isolation makes one-offs, and the copycat game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Australia&rsquo;s oddballs, one laugh at a time.',
  what:'Australia&rsquo;s birds grew up on an island continent, far from everywhere: the emu runs instead of flying, the kookaburra laughs, the cassowary wears a helmet, and the lyrebird is the world&rsquo;s best mimic.',
  mean:'Isolation is the idea: cut off for millions of years, Australia evolved birds that exist nowhere else — evolution&rsquo;s experiment island.',
  know:'The emu stands taller than a grown-up and sprints on giant legs; the kookaburra&rsquo;s call sounds like wild laughter at dusk; the cassowary has a helmet-casque and a dagger claw — the most dangerous bird alive; the lyrebird copies chainsaws, car alarms and every bird it meets.',
  why:'The lyrebird is the animal kingdom&rsquo;s sound recorder — children who love copying sounds have found their champion.',
  dev:'Sound imitation and memory, island-biology thinking, and respect-for-distance with the cassowary (admire, never approach).',
  vocab:'Australia, emu, kookaburra, laugh, cassowary, helmet, lyrebird, mimic, copy, call',
  teach:'1) The emu: the runner — a giant flightless bird on enormous legs, dad raises the striped chicks.\n2) The kookaburra: the laugher — its dusk call rolls like a laugh across the whole valley; it is the "laughing bird" of every Australian story.\n3) The cassowary: the helmet — a hard casque tops its head and one dagger claw can be seriously dangerous: admire from far.\n4) The lyrebird: the copycat — it builds its song from borrowed sounds: other birds, camera shutters, car alarms, chainsaws. Perfect copies.\n5) The island idea: find Australia on the map — its birds had millions of years alone to become one-offs.',
  say:'"Australian birds are one-of-a-kind! The emu runs taller than a grown-up, the kookaburra laughs at the sunset, the cassowary wears a helmet, and the lyrebird copies EVERYTHING — other birds, car alarms, chainsaws. Australia is the birds&rsquo; experiment island!"',
  notice:'On the card, the emu stands tallest, the kookaburra grips its branch, the cassowary shows its helmet and heavy legs, and the lyrebird carries its spectacular tail. Which two gave up flying for running?',
  ask:'"Which bird laughs?" (the kookaburra) "Which bird can copy a chainsaw?" (the lyrebird) "Which bird should we never approach?" (the cassowary — admire from far)',
  childSay:'Your child might laugh-call at every sunset for a week, copy the doorbell, the dog and the kettle in one breath "like a lyrebird", or guard the cassowary card with a "dangerous — look only" sign.',
  real:'Kookaburra calls are the sound of every Australian evening — and the old song "Kookaburra sits in the old gum tree" is this card&rsquo;s soundtrack. Lyrebird videos of real chainsaw mimicry are the best five minutes on the internet.',
  play:'Copycat concert: one person makes three sounds in a row (clap, whistle, hum), the lyrebird must repeat them exactly — increasing to five. Then everyone learns the kookaburra song together.',
  check:'Ask: "What does the kookaburra&rsquo;s call sound like?" (laughter) "What does the lyrebird do?" (copies any sound perfectly) "What sits on the cassowary&rsquo;s head?" (a hard helmet — the casque)'},
 'coastal-animals':{
  title:'The Coastal Animals Lesson',one:'Life where the land meets the sea.',
  seoTitle:'Coastal Animals Lesson for Kids — Seal, Sea Lion, Pelican and Puffin, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 3+: coastal animals — seal, sea lion, pelican and puffin. The ear-flap tell that separates seals from sea lions, the pelican pouch and the beach-moves game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the shore crew and its easiest ID trick, one wave at a time.',
  what:'Coastal animals live at the meeting line of land and sea: the seal, the sea lion, the pelican and the puffin — half their life on rocks, half in the waves.',
  know:'Sea lions have little ear flaps and can walk on all four flippers; true seals have no ear flaps and scoot on their bellies; pelican pouches stretch to hold several liters of fish-and-water; puffins fly underwater with their wings and carry fish crosswise in their beaks.',
  why:'Beach days are seal-and-pelican days in much of the US, UK and Canada — the card turns every coastal walk into a who&rsquo;s-who.',
  mean:'Two worlds, one animal: the shore crew breathes air, raises babies on land, and hunts in the sea — the beach is their doorstep.',
  dev:'Observation and ID by detail (ear flaps!), body-movement imitation, and beach ethics: watch from a distance, never chase a resting seal.',
  vocab:'coast, seal, sea lion, ear flaps, pelican, pouch, puffin, colony, rocks, tide',
  teach:'1) The seal: the belly-slider — no ear flaps, and on land it wriggles like a caterpillar; in water it is a torpedo.\n2) The sea lion: the walker — ear flaps and rotating back flippers let it "walk"; it barks like a dog and claps on cue.\n3) The pelican: the pouch — scoop, drain, swallow: the pouch is a net and a lunchbox. Pelicans fly in lines and glide low over the waves.\n4) The puffin: the underwater parrot — wings do both jobs, and the beak carries a whole row of fish crosswise for the chick.\n5) The shore rule: a sleeping seal is off-duty — give it room, keep dogs leashed, and report stranded pups to a ranger.',
  say:'"Coastal animals live on the beach&rsquo;s doorstep! The seal slides, the sea lion walks and barks, the pelican scoops fish in its pouch, and the puffin flies underwater. How to tell the seal cousins: look for the ear flaps!"',
  notice:'On the card, the seal lies smooth and earless, the sea lion props up on its flippers, the pelican shows the famous pouch, and the puffin wears its rainbow beak. Spot the ear flaps — that is the tell.',
  ask:'"How do you tell a seal from a sea lion?" (ear flaps — sea lions have them) "What does the pelican use its pouch for?" (scooping fish like a net) "How does the puffin fly underwater?" (with its wings)',
  childSay:'Your child might wriggle across every rug "seal-style", bark-and-clap like a sea lion on request, or pouch-scoop toys from the bath announcing "pelican!"',
  real:'Seal colonies haul out on coasts on both sides of the Atlantic — boardwalks and ranger stations mark the viewing spots. Puffin islands in the UK and Maine welcome boat trips in summer.',
  play:'Beach moves: seal wriggle on a rug, sea lion walk-and-bark, pelican scoop from the blue-carpet sea, puffin flap-splash. Finish with the ID quiz: ear flaps or no ear flaps?',
  check:'Ask: "Which one has ear flaps?" (the sea lion) "How does the seal move on land?" (wriggling on its belly) "How many fish can a puffin carry at once?" (a whole row, crosswise)'},
 'river-animals':{
  title:'The River Animals Lesson',one:'The moving road and its crew.',
  seoTitle:'River Animals Lesson for Kids — Otter, Beaver, River Dolphin and Crocodile, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 3+: river animals — otter, beaver, river dolphin and crocodile. The dam that builds a neighborhood, the pink dolphin and the alligator-vs-crocodile snout tell.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the river&rsquo;s crew, one bend at a time.',
  what:'A river is a moving road with its own residents: the playful otter, the building beaver, the pink river dolphin and the patient crocodile.',
  know:'Otters slide for fun and hold hands while floating; beaver dams slow floods and create ponds where fish, frogs and ducks move in; river dolphins are born grey and turn pink; crocodiles float like logs with eyes above water.',
  mean:'One animal can build a world: the beaver&rsquo;s dam is a whole habitat — the deck&rsquo;s clearest "animals change the land" lesson.',
  why:'Rivers run through every town — and the card crew lives in rivers on five continents.',
  dev:'Engineering play (dams that actually hold), playing-for-fun biology (otters), and the snout-shape tell for crocodilians.',
  vocab:'river, otter, slide, beaver, dam, lodge, river dolphin, pink, crocodile, bank',
  teach:'1) The otter: the player — mudbank slides, somersaults, and hand-holding rafts so nobody drifts off in their sleep.\n2) The beaver: the builder — teeth that never stop growing, so it fells trees, stacks dams, and the pond behind becomes a new neighborhood with a lodge front door under water.\n3) The river dolphin: the pink one — born grey, turning pink with age and sun; it swims through flooded forest where sonar matters more than eyes.\n4) The crocodile: the lurker — a floating log with eyes; the snout tell: crocodiles have pointy V-snouts, alligators wide U-snouts.\n5) The river rule: the water moves — everything on this card is built for current, from otter rudders to crocodile tails.',
  say:'"The river has a crew! The otter plays and slides, the beaver builds a dam and a lodge, the river dolphin swims pink through the trees, and the crocodile floats like a log with eyes. Pointy snout? Crocodile. Round snout? Alligator!"',
  notice:'On the card, the otter curls with its whiskers forward, the beaver shows flat tail and gnawing teeth, the river dolphin arcs above the water, and the crocodile lines the bank. Which one built something on this card?',
  ask:'"What does the beaver&rsquo;s dam make?" (a pond — a new home for fish, frogs and ducks) "What color is an old river dolphin?" (pink) "Which has the pointy V-snout?" (the crocodile)',
  childSay:'Your child might slide down every smooth hallway "otter-style", engineer a sock-dam in the doorway with real structural pride, or snout-sort every toy crocodilian into V and U teams.',
  real:'Beaver dams are roadside sights across Canada and the northern US; otters have returned to clean rivers in every one of the three countries — a famous conservation win children can visit.',
  play:'Dam builders: cushions across a doorway, then a storm of rolled socks — hold the line or rebuild stronger. Then the otter slide: socks on a smooth floor, run, and slide. Play IS the biology.',
  check:'Ask: "Why does the beaver build dams?" (a safe pond for its lodge — and a new home for others) "What does the otter do for fun?" (slides) "How do you tell a crocodile from an alligator?" (pointy V-snout vs round U-snout)'},
 'wetland-animals':{
  title:'The Wetland Animals Lesson',one:'The marsh of patient legs.',
  seoTitle:'Wetland Animals Lesson for Kids — Heron, Stork, Alligator and Muskrat, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: wetland animals — heron, stork, alligator and muskrat. The sponge that soaks floods, the statue-still heron hunt and the heron freeze game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the marsh crew, one still moment at a time.',
  what:'Wetlands are the soggy lands between water and dry ground — marshes, swamps and reed beds — and their animals are built for waiting: the heron, the stork, the alligator and the muskrat.',
  know:'Herons can stand statue-still for minutes and sometimes fish with bait; storks strut on stilt legs and ride thermals like raptors; alligators rule the warm marshes and "bellow" so loud their backs vibrate; muskrats paddle with a rudder tail and build reed lodges.',
  mean:'Wetlands are the world&rsquo;s sponge and filter: they soak up floods and clean the water — protecting wetlands protects whole towns downstream.',
  why:'Every reedy pond is a wetland in miniature: herons fish them in the US, UK and Canada, and this card is the field guide.',
  dev:'Patience as a skill (the heron lesson), wetland science in one word (sponge), and quiet-watching practice.',
  vocab:'wetland, marsh, heron, stork, alligator, muskrat, reeds, patience, sponge, bellow',
  teach:'1) The heron: the statue — one long leg-lift, then stone-still until the fish forgets it is a bird. Some herons drop bread crumbs as fishing bait.\n2) The stork: the strutter — stilt legs wade deeper water, and it soars on thermals between continents.\n3) The alligator: the bellower — it rules warm marshes, floats like the crocodile&rsquo;s cousin, and its bellow makes its whole back ripple.\n4) The muskrat: the paddler — a round-tailed swimmer that builds a reed lodge like a beaver&rsquo;s smaller cousin.\n5) The sponge idea: a wetland soaks up heavy rain and lets it go slowly — the marsh is the town&rsquo;s umbrella and its cup.',
  say:'"The wetland runs on patience! The heron stands like a statue — one fish, please. The stork struts on stilts, the alligator bellows till its back ripples, and the muskrat paddles its round-tailed boat. And the marsh itself? The town&rsquo;s sponge!"',
  notice:'On the card, the heron folds like an umbrella handle, the stork stands tallest, the alligator shows the U-snout and the muskrat paddles its round tail. Which two hunt by standing still?',
  ask:'"How does the heron fish?" (stands perfectly still, then snaps) "What does a wetland do for a town?" (soaks up floods like a sponge) "What does the alligator&rsquo;s bellow do?" (booms so loud its back vibrates)',
  childSay:'Your child might freeze mid-step all over the house "heron mode", bellow with a vibrating back (that is the whole point), or build a reed lodge for the bath toys.',
  real:'Herons stand in park ponds in every US, UK and Canadian city — five quiet minutes at the reeds usually earns a strike. Alligator wetlands are the famous sights of Florida and Louisiana.',
  play:'Heron statue game: freeze for ten whole seconds, then one lightning neck-snap at a sock-fish. Then the sponge test: two trays, one with a "marsh" of sponges, one bare — pour the storm and see which floods.',
  check:'Ask: "What is the heron&rsquo;s hunting style?" (statue-still waiting) "What does a wetland soak up?" (flood water — like a sponge) "What does the muskrat build?" (a reed lodge)'},
 'crustaceans':{
  title:'The Crustaceans Lesson',one:'Shells outside, legs everywhere.',
  seoTitle:'Crustaceans Lesson for Kids — Lobster, Shrimp, Crayfish and Barnacle, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: crustaceans — lobster, shrimp (prawn in Britain), crayfish and barnacle. The two-claw secret, the backwards snap and the head-first barnacle, with crab-walk tag.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the armored leg-team, one shell at a time.',
  what:'Crustaceans are the shell-backed animals with too many legs to count quickly: lobster, shrimp, crayfish and barnacle — joints, armor and underwater engineering.',
  know:'A lobster&rsquo;s two claws do two different jobs — crusher and snipper; shrimp swim backwards with a tail-snap; crayfish are freshwater lobsters; barnacles glue themselves head-first to rocks and kick food into their mouths with their FEET.',
  why:'The prawn/shrimp pair rides here: same little swimmer, different names across the ocean — the deck&rsquo;s tastiest word lesson.',
  mean:'Armor is the family idea: the skeleton is on the OUTSIDE, so growing up means molting — climbing out of your own shell, soft and new, then hardening again.',
  dev:'Counting beyond ten legs, the molting idea (growing pains, crustacean edition), and word-geography through shrimp/prawn.',
  vocab:'crustacean, lobster, claw, shrimp, prawn, crayfish, barnacle, molt, shell, armor',
  teach:'1) The armor idea: crustaceans wear their skeleton outside — protection first, flexibility second.\n2) The lobster: two claws, two jobs — the big crusher cracks, the small snipper cuts. It takes five to seven years to grow catch-size.\n3) The shrimp: the escape artist — the tail-flick snaps it backwards at speed. Americans say shrimp, the British say prawn: same swimmer.\n4) The crayfish: the freshwater twin — lobster-shaped, lives under river rocks, and walks forward but swims backward too.\n5) The barnacle: the stander-still — glued head-first in one spot forever, kicking plankton into its mouth with its feet. The tide does the shopping.',
  say:'"Crustaceans wear their bones on the outside! The lobster has a crusher claw and a snipper claw, the shrimp snaps backwards to escape, the crayfish hides under river rocks, and the barnacle stands on its head eating with its feet! And prawn or shrimp — both names are right."',
  notice:'On the card, the lobster raises both claws, the shrimp curves for its tail-snap, the crayfish hunkers low, and the barnacles cluster like little volcanoes. Which one never moves again after growing up?',
  ask:'"What are the lobster&rsquo;s two claws for?" (crushing and snipping — two different jobs) "How does a shrimp escape?" (tail-snap, backwards) "What is a shrimp called in Britain?" (a prawn)',
  childSay:'Your child might claw-sort the cutlery drawer into crushers and snippers, tail-flick backwards off the couch "shrimp escape!", or fact-drop barnacle feet at the aquarium.',
  real:'Tide pools and harbor walls in all three countries grow real barnacles — feel the rough volcano shells on any pier leg. Lobster fishing is a famous sight in Maine and Atlantic Canada.',
  play:'Crab-walk tag: scuttle sideways, and a tag freezes you in a shrimp-tail curl until a friend "molts" you free. Then the two-claw game: one hand crushes (gently!), one hand snips — sort pompoms by size.',
  check:'Ask: "Where is a crustacean&rsquo;s skeleton?" (on the outside — a shell) "What do the lobster&rsquo;s two claws do?" (crush and snip) "What does the barnacle eat with?" (its feet)'},
 'shellfish':{
  title:'The Shellfish Lesson',one:'Animals that carry their houses.',
  seoTitle:'Shellfish Lesson for Kids — Clam, Mussel, Oyster and Nautilus, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: shellfish — clam, mussel, oyster and nautilus. How pearls really grow, the mussel&rsquo;s strong threads and the nautilus submarine, with shell sorting.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the shell-dwellers, one hinge at a time.',
  what:'Shellfish are the soft animals inside hard shells: clams buried in sand, mussels glued in bunches, oysters making pearls, and the nautilus steering its spiral through deep water.',
  know:'Clams dig fast with a muscular foot; mussels tie themselves down with byssus threads — nature&rsquo;s ropes; oysters wrap an annoying grain of sand in thousands of pearly layers — that is a pearl; the nautilus changes its buoyancy to rise and sink like a submarine and has barely changed in 500 million years.',
  mean:'Home is the idea: a shell is a house, a shield and a hinge — and each of the four carries a different model.',
  why:'Beach shells are every child&rsquo;s first collection — this card tells them whose house each one was.',
  dev:'Collection science (sorting shells by shape), slow-growth wonder (pearls take years), and the ancient-survivor story.',
  vocab:'shellfish, clam, mussel, oyster, pearl, nautilus, spiral, hinge, tide, filter',
  teach:'1) The clam: the digger — a strong foot anchors, then yanks it down to safety; it spits little fountains at the tide.\n2) The mussel: the roper — sticky threads glue it to rocks in big hanging families, so the waves cannot take it.\n3) The oyster: the pearl-maker — sand in the shell is annoying, so the oyster wraps it in smooth nacre, layer after layer, for years. A pearl is patience.\n4) The nautilus: the submarine — it pumps water in and out of its shell chambers to rise and sink; its spiral has barely changed in 500 million years.\n5) The filter idea: all these animals sip water and strain food from it — a whole bay of shellfish filters the sea like a kettle of tiny cleaners.',
  say:'"Shellfish carry their houses! The clam digs, the mussel ropes itself to the rocks, the oyster wraps sand in pearly layers until — a pearl! And the nautilus steers its spiral like a 500-million-year-old submarine."',
  notice:'On the card, the clam gapes in its ridged shell, the mussel shows the dark blue-black shell, the oyster opens rough and grey, and the nautilus unrolls its perfect spiral with tentacles at the door. Which one is a perfect spiral?',
  ask:'"How does a pearl begin?" (a grain of sand the oyster wraps in pearly layers) "How does the mussel hold on?" (strong little threads) "What does the nautilus do with its shell?" (rises and sinks like a submarine)',
  childSay:'Your child might sort the beach bucket into clams, mussels and "the spiral one", wrap a pebble in layer after layer of tissue paper "making a pearl", or refuse to throw back any nautilus drawing.',
  real:'Mussel beds hang on every rocky shore at low tide; pearl farms welcome visitors in some regions; the nautilus lives in deep Indo-Pacific water — aquariums are the reliable sighting.',
  play:'Shell sorting bowl: spiral, fan, flat — sort real shells or pasta shapes into three houses. Then the pearl patience game: wrap a pebble in 20 layers of tissue, one at a time, and open it a week later.',
  check:'Ask: "What is a pearl made of?" (layered nacre wrapped around sand) "Which shellfish makes ropes?" (the mussel — byssus threads) "Which one is a living fossil?" (the nautilus)'},
 'deep-sea-animals':{
  title:'The Deep-Sea Animals Lesson',one:'Life where the sun never reaches.',
  seoTitle:'Deep-Sea Animals Lesson for Kids — Anglerfish, Giant Squid, Manta Ray and Sea Turtle, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: deep-sea animals — anglerfish, giant squid, manta ray and sea turtle. Making your own light, the biggest eyes in the ocean and the turtle&rsquo;s magnetic compass.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the midnight ocean, one glow at a time.',
  what:'Deep below the waves, sunlight fails — and the animals answer with inventions: glowing lures, dinner-plate eyes, flying wings and magnetic maps.',
  know:'The anglerfish fishes with a glowing lure powered by friendly bacteria; the giant squid has the largest eyes in the animal kingdom; the manta ray "flies" with wing-flaps like a giant bird; sea turtles cross whole oceans and navigate by Earth&rsquo;s magnetic field, returning to their birth beach.',
  mean:'Darkness is an opportunity: where the sun quits, life builds its own light — the deep sea is a working laboratory.',
  why:'The deep sea is the least-explored place on Earth — more people have walked on the Moon than have visited its floor. This card is the frontier.',
  dev:'Bioluminescence as first chemistry wow, scale comparison (eyes bigger than dinner plates), and navigation science.',
  vocab:'deep sea, dark, anglerfish, lure, glow, giant squid, manta ray, sea turtle, navigate, compass',
  teach:'1) The light rule: below a few hundred meters, the sun is gone — any light down there is made by the animals.\n2) The anglerfish: the fisherman — a glowing bulb on its forehead lures curious fish straight to its teeth.\n3) The giant squid: the giant eye — the size of a dinner plate, catching every scrap of light a mile down.\n4) The manta ray: the flyer — wings wider than a car flap like a bird; mantas somersault-feed through plankton clouds.\n5) The sea turtle: the navigator — it senses Earth&rsquo;s magnetism like an invisible map, swims whole oceans, and lays its eggs on the beach where it hatched.',
  say:'"The deep sea is dark — so the animals bring their own light! The anglerfish dangles a glowing fishing rod, the giant squid has eyes like dinner plates, the manta ray flies on giant wings, and the sea turtle navigates the whole ocean by a magnetic map!"',
  notice:'On the card, the anglerfish glows above its grin, the giant squid curls its long arms, the manta ray spreads wider than its square, and the sea turtle paddles beside them. Which one is built like an airplane?',
  ask:'"Why does the anglerfish glow?" (its lure invites dinner in the dark) "Which animal has the biggest eyes?" (the giant squid) "How does the sea turtle find its birth beach?" (Earth&rsquo;s magnetic field — an invisible compass)',
  childSay:'Your child might fish for paper clips with a tea-light "lure" in every dim room, measure dinner plates against their head "squid eyes", or magnet-walk the house narrating turtle navigation.',
  real:'Glow-in-the-dark plankton waves are real on some coasts — paddle at night and the water lights up. Sea-turtle hatching nights on guarded beaches let families watch the babies race to the moon.',
  play:'Glow angler game: tape a battery tea-light to a stick, dim the room, and "fish" for paper fish with paperclip mouths. Then manta wings: glide across the room flapping slow, somersault-feed at the middle.',
  check:'Ask: "What light is there in the deep sea?" (only the light animals make) "How does the anglerfish hunt?" (a glowing lure) "What does the sea turtle use to navigate?" (Earth&rsquo;s magnetism)'},
 'pollinators':{
  title:'The Pollinators Lesson',one:'The carriers of the yellow dust.',
  seoTitle:'Pollinators Lesson for Kids — Honeybee, Bumblebee, Hummingbird and Bat, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 3+: pollinators — honeybee, bumblebee, hummingbird and bat. How pollen becomes fruit, the waggle dance and the pollination-tag game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the flower-friends, one transfer at a time.',
  what:'Pollinators carry pollen from flower to flower as they sip nectar — and that yellow dust is what lets plants make fruit and seeds. One in three bites of food exists because of them.',
  mean:'Partnership is the idea: the flower pays in nectar, the pollinator pays in delivery — nobody invoices anybody, and the world eats.',
  know:'Honeybees tell the hive where flowers are with a waggle dance; bumblebees fly in colder weather than honeybees; hummingbirds hover and beat wings 50+ times a second; bats pollinate the night flowers — bananas, mangoes, agave.',
  why:'The apple in the lunchbox is the proof: a bee visited an apple blossom in spring, or there is no apple in autumn.',
  dev:'Partnership thinking (two-sided deals in nature), dance-as-language, and garden citizenship: plant flowers, feed the crew.',
  vocab:'pollinator, pollen, nectar, honeybee, waggle dance, bumblebee, hummingbird, hover, bat, blossom',
  teach:'1) The deal: the flower offers a sip of sweet nectar; the visitor takes pollen dust on its body and delivers it to the next flower. Deal done — fruit begins.\n2) The honeybee: the dancer — its waggle dance tells the hive the direction AND distance of the flowers. A living map, in dance.\n3) The bumblebee: the cold-morning specialist — bigger, furrier, and it can fly when honeybees stay home.\n4) The hummingbird: the hoverer — 50-plus wingbeats a second, sipping backwards out of the flower it just entered.\n5) The bat: the night shift — it pollinates bananas, mangoes and agave after dark; the day crew sleeps through it.',
  say:'"Pollinators carry the yellow dust from flower to flower — and that is how fruit begins! The honeybee dances the directions, the bumblebee flies in the cold, the hummingbird hovers, and the bat works the night shift. Thank you, pollinators — the fruit is on the table!"',
  notice:'On the card, the honeybee and bumblebee hover at the flowers, the hummingbird dips its long beak, and the bat spreads its wings at the night bloom. Two of the four fly by day, one by twilight, one by night — sort them.',
  ask:'"What do pollinators carry between flowers?" (pollen) "How does the honeybee tell the hive about flowers?" (the waggle dance) "Which pollinator works at night?" (the bat)',
  childSay:'Your child might waggle-dance the route to the kitchen, hover-drink from a cup with a straw "hummingbird style", or guard every bee they meet with "it is working, do not bother it".',
  real:'Planting a pot of bee-friendly flowers (lavender, borage) invites the whole card to the balcony. Beekeepers&rsquo; waggle-dance videos are the card&rsquo;s living proof.',
  play:'Pollination tag: press pollen-dots (sticky notes) onto two fingers, move them flower-cup to flower-cup, and every completed route earns a fruit sticker on the bowl. The table eats when the pollinators finish.',
  check:'Ask: "Why do flowers give nectar?" (to pay the pollinator for carrying pollen) "What is the honeybee&rsquo;s dance for?" (telling the hive where the flowers are) "Name a fruit pollinated by bats." (bananas or mangoes)'},
 'invertebrates':{
  title:'The Invertebrates Lesson',one:'The animals without backbones.',
  seoTitle:'Invertebrates Lesson for Kids — Octopus, Earthworm, Snail and Starfish, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: invertebrates — octopus, earthworm, snail and starfish. Nine brains and three hearts, the no-bones truth and the no-bones obstacle course.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the backbonless majority, one wonder at a time.',
  what:'Invertebrates are animals with no backbone — and they are nearly all of the animals on Earth: the octopus, the earthworm, the snail and the starfish are four of the cleverest.',
  know:'The octopus has nine brains, three hearts and taste-sensors in its suckers; worms breathe through their skin; snails carry spiral homes; sea stars regrow lost arms and walk on hundreds of tube feet.',
  mean:'The backbone is the rare thing: about 97 of every 100 animal species manage perfectly without one — soft bodies, hard results.',
  why:'This card quietly flips a child&rsquo;s picture of the animal kingdom: the squishy ones are the majority, and the octopus is the smartest animal they will ever meet on a card.',
  dev:'Classification (vertebrate vs invertebrate), respect-for-different-bodies, and slime-science through worm and snail play.',
  vocab:'invertebrate, backbone, octopus, sucker, earthworm, skin, snail, spiral, sea star, tube feet',
  teach:'1) The test: touch your back — bones. That is the vertebrate club, and it is the MINORITY club.\n2) The octopus: nine brains (one central, one per arm), three hearts, and suckers that taste what they touch. It can change color and shape in a second.\n3) The earthworm: the recycler — it eats fallen leaves and turns them into soil, breathing through its damp skin the whole shift.\n4) The snail: the house-carrier — spiral shell, one muscular foot, and eyes on the tips of its feelers.\n5) The sea star: the regenerator — lose an arm, grow it back; each arm-tip is packed with tube feet that grip and pull.',
  say:'"Invertebrates have no backbone — and they are almost ALL the animals! The octopus has nine brains and three hearts, the worm recycles the garden, the snail carries a spiral house, and the sea star regrows its arms. Bones are optional in the animal kingdom!"',
  notice:'On the card, the octopus shows all eight arms, the earthworm curves its simple body, the snail&rsquo;s shell spirals tight, and the sea star spreads five arms. Only ONE of the four could ever be your size — which, and why not?',
  ask:'"Do most animals have backbones?" (no — invertebrates are almost all of them) "How many hearts does an octopus have?" (three) "What can the sea star regrow?" (a lost arm)',
  childSay:'Your child might count their own backbone vertebrae and find it "showing off", worm-crawl the hallway, or declare the octopus the smartest animal in the house and quiz everyone on why.',
  real:'Touch tanks at aquariums let children feel a sea star&rsquo;s tube feet grip their palm; worm-composting bins in classrooms show the recycling live.',
  play:'No-bones obstacle course: worm-slither under a table, snail-glide the slow stretch, octopus-curl eight arms through a doorway, sea-star reach on the finish line. Bones optional; finishing mandatory.',
  check:'Ask: "What does invertebrate mean?" (no backbone) "How many brains does the octopus have?" (nine — one per arm plus the boss) "Why do worms come out in the rain?" (damp skin breathes better; tunnels flood)'},
 'spiders-and-ticks':{
  title:'The Spiders and Ticks Lesson',one:'Eight legs, two body parts.',
  seoTitle:'Spiders and Ticks Lesson for Kids — Spider, Tarantula, Tick and Harvestman, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: spiders and ticks — spider, tarantula, tick and harvestman. Arachnids vs insects, silk stronger than steel, and the kind tick-check habit.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the arachnids and the one calm habit they ask of us, one leg-pair at a time.',
  what:'Spiders and ticks are arachnids — eight legs, two body parts, no antennae: the web-spinning spider, the fuzzy tarantula, the waiting tick and the stilt-legged harvestman.',
  mean:'Not insects: the six-leg club and the eight-leg club are different families — counting legs is the whole ID course.',
  know:'Spider silk is stronger than steel of the same weight; tarantulas are shy and mostly harmless to people; ticks wait in tall grass and need calm, careful removal; harvestmen (daddy longlegs) are NOT spiders — one body piece, no silk, no venom.',
  why:'This card carries the deck&rsquo;s practical safety lesson, taught kindly: the after-walk tick check is a five-second habit for a lifetime.',
  dev:'Counting legs for classification, silk engineering respect, and calm-safety habits without fear.',
  vocab:'arachnid, spider, silk, web, tarantula, tick, harvestman, eight legs, check, tweezers',
  teach:'1) The count: insects six legs, arachnids eight — count on the card, count on every bug forever.\n2) The spider: the engineer — silk comes from its own body, spun into webs that catch dinner; silk thread is stronger than steel of the same weight.\n3) The tarantula: the gentle giant — hairy, slow, and it would rather hide than bother anyone; its hairs are its defense, not its bite.\n4) The tick: the waiter — it climbs a grass blade and waits for a ride; the kind habit after walks: a quick tick check, and a grown-up removes any tick slowly with fine tweezers.\n5) The harvestman: the not-spider — one body piece, no silk, no venom; it strolls on stilt legs eating tiny bugs.',
  say:'"Spiders and ticks are arachnids — eight legs, two body parts! The spider spins silk stronger than steel, the tarantula is a gentle giant, the harvestman is not a spider at all, and the tick waits in the grass — so after our walks: the tick check!"',
  notice:'On the card, the spider shows the classic round body, the tarantula wears fuzzy legs, the tick is small and flat, and the harvestman balances on stilt legs. Count the legs on all four — always eight.',
  ask:'"How many legs does an arachnid have?" (eight) "Is the harvestman a spider?" (no — one body piece, no silk) "What do we do after a walk in tall grass?" (the tick check — gently, with a grown-up)',
  childSay:'Your child might leg-count every bug in the yard with new authority, wind a "web" of wool between chairs and test it with pom-poms, or run the tick check like a fire drill — calm, quick, done.',
  real:'Real webs hold dew at sunrise on every lawn — the strongest silk demonstration is free. Tick season runs spring to autumn in the US, UK and Canada; the habit is the same everywhere.',
  play:'Web engineering: wind wool between chair legs with three gap sizes, then toss pom-pom bugs — which gap catches? Then the calm tick-check role-play: checked head to toe in thirty seconds, all clear, sticker.',
  check:'Ask: "What is spider silk stronger than?" (steel of the same weight) "How many body parts does an arachnid have?" (two) "Who removes a tick?" (a grown-up, slowly, with fine tweezers)'},
 'dog-family':{
  title:'The Dog Family Lesson',one:'From wolf to your sofa.',
  seoTitle:'The Dog Family Lesson for Kids — Wolf, Coyote, Arctic Fox and Dingo, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 3+: the dog family — wolf, coyote, arctic fox and dingo. Packs as families, the arctic fox&rsquo;s coat change and the pack howl game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the canine cousins, one howl at a time.',
  what:'The dog family runs the wild on every continent: the wolf, the coyote, the arctic fox and the dingo — and the family pet at the end of the sofa.',
  know:'A wolf pack is a family — parents and their pups hunting together; coyotes thrive near towns on their cleverness; the arctic fox changes from brown summer fur to white winter fur; dingoes are Australia&rsquo;s wild dogs, arrived thousands of years ago.',
  mean:'Family is the family idea: dogs do everything as a team — hunting, guarding, raising pups — and the pet at home still speaks the family language.',
  why:'Every child with a dog (or a dream of one) is holding the family tree in their lap: this card is where the pet comes from.',
  dev:'Family-structure thinking, coat-change adaptation, and the social-howl as belonging practice.',
  vocab:'dog family, wolf, pack, coyote, arctic fox, dingo, howl, hunt, cousin, loyal',
  teach:'1) The wolf: the pack leader&rsquo;s pack — a family unit: mother, father, the pups of past years. They hunt together and howl to say "we are here, together".\n2) The coyote: the adapter — smaller, cleverer, thriving near towns where wolves cannot; the twilight songdog.\n3) The arctic fox: the coat-changer — brown in summer, snow-white in winter, and it survives nights of minus fifty.\n4) The dingo: the Australian — wild dogs that arrived by boat with people long ago and went native.\n5) The pet: the family&rsquo;s smallest member — every good boy descends from wolves, and the tail wag still means what it meant in the pack.',
  say:'"The dog family runs the wild together! Wolves hunt as a family and howl — arooooo — the coyote sings at dusk, the arctic fox changes to a white winter coat, and the dingo rules Australia. Your dog at home? Same family — the sofa branch!"',
  notice:'On the card, the wolf stands tall, the coyote looks lean and quick, the arctic fox is round with tiny ears, and the dingo is the yellowish runner. Which two live where it snows?',
  ask:'"What is a wolf pack?" (a family — parents and pups, together) "What does the arctic fox&rsquo;s coat do?" (brown in summer, white in winter) "Where do dingoes live?" (Australia)',
  childSay:'Your child might howl the family roll-call at bedtime, pack-walk every toy dog in a line "together, together", or point at the pet and announce "wolf technology".',
  real:'Wolf howls are real in Yellowstone and Canadian parks — ranger-led evening walks hear them live. Coyotes now live in most North American cities; the arctic fox summers in every northern zoo.',
  play:'Pack howl: one at a time, then all together — the family chorus, exactly like a pack greeting the moon. Then the patrol: walk the yard as one line, nobody ahead, nobody behind.',
  check:'Ask: "What is a wolf pack made of?" (a family) "Why does the pack howl?" ("we are here, together") "Which family member lives on your sofa?" (the dog — descended from wolves)'},
 'deer-and-grazers':{
  title:'The Deer and Grazers Lesson',one:'The grass-eaters with the running hooves.',
  seoTitle:'Deer and Grazers Lesson for Kids — Moose, Reindeer, Bison and Antelope, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: deer and grazers — moose, reindeer, bison and antelope. The reindeer/caribou and moose/elk name pairs, antler regrowth and the grazer lunch.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the big grazers and their ocean-crossing names, one hoof at a time.',
  what:'Deer and grazers are the hoofed plant-eaters: the moose, the reindeer, the bison and the antelope — built to eat grass and leaves all day and outrun everything that notices.',
  know:'Only male deer grow antlers — except reindeer, where both sexes do; the moose is the biggest deer on Earth; bison are the shaggy heavyweights of the American plains; antelope are the sprinters.',
  why:'This card carries two famous name pairs: the reindeer of Europe is the caribou of Canada, and the moose is called the elk in Britain — same animals, two names, both right.',
  mean:'Built-for-the-job is the idea: flat teeth for grinding grass, hooves for running, antlers and horns for the season&rsquo;s arguments.',
  dev:'Word-geography (caribou, elk), plant-eater anatomy (flat grinding teeth), and the antler regrowth cycle.',
  vocab:'grazer, moose, elk, reindeer, caribou, bison, antelope, antlers, hooves, herd',
  teach:'1) The moose: the giant — the tallest wild animal in the northern forest, with a wide antler crown it grows and sheds every year. In Britain, the same animal is called the elk.\n2) The reindeer: the traveler — the reindeer of Lapland is the caribou of Canada and Alaska: same animal, two names. Both males AND females grow antlers — the girls pull the sled too.\n3) The bison: the heavyweight — a shaggy wall of muscle that once numbered in the tens of millions on the plains.\n4) The antelope: the sprinter — built like a spring; some can outrun a cheetah over distance.\n5) The grazer&rsquo;s mouth: flat grinding teeth at the back for grass all day — say cheese with your molars and feel the difference.',
  say:'"Deer and grazers eat grass all day and run fast! The moose is the giant of the forest — the elk, in Britain. The reindeer is the caribou in Canada — same animal, two names! The bison is a shaggy wall, and the antelope bounces like the grass is a trampoline!"',
  notice:'On the card, the moose carries the wide flat antlers, the reindeer has the branching rack, the bison hunches shaggy and big, and the antelope stands slim with spiral horns. Two grow antlers that fall off — which two?',
  ask:'"What is a reindeer called in Canada?" (caribou) "What is a moose called in Britain?" (the elk) "Which grazers grow NEW antlers every year?" (the moose and the reindeer — both sexes for reindeer)',
  childSay:'Your child might insist the sled stories star "caribou, actually", graze the vegetable plate like a herbivore, or gallop the hallway announcing antler status.',
  real:'Reindeer herding is a living culture across Norway, Sweden and Finland; wild caribou herds migrate across Canada in numbers that stop traffic. Bison herds roam Yellowstone — the traffic jam is famous.',
  play:'Grazer lunch: spinach, celery and carrots, eaten slowly, grazing-style, molars only. Then the migration walk: the family walks a big slow loop of the yard, exactly like the herd, with one lookout.',
  check:'Ask: "Name the two names for the same northern deer." (reindeer and caribou) "What do grazers eat all day?" (grass and leaves — flat teeth grind it) "What happens to moose antlers?" (grown and shed every year)'},
 'rodents':{
  title:'The Rodents Lesson',one:'The teeth that never stop.',
  seoTitle:'Rodents Lesson for Kids — Squirrel, Porcupine, Chinchilla and Prairie Dog, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: rodents — squirrel, porcupine, chinchilla and prairie dog. The gnawing teeth that never stop growing, dust-bath chinchillas and the tunnel-town game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the world&rsquo;s biggest mammal family, one gnaw at a time.',
  what:'Rodents are the gnawers — front teeth that grow their whole lives, so they chew their whole lives: the squirrel, the porcupine, the chinchilla and the prairie dog.',
  know:'Squirrels plant forests by forgetting acorns; porcupines wear some 30,000 quills (modified hairs); chinchilla fur is so dense water cannot reach the skin — they bathe in dust; prairie dogs build towns of tunnels with rooms and sentries.',
  mean:'The tooth is the family trick: gnawing keeps the ever-growing teeth short — and gnawing reshapes forests, fields and riverbanks.',
  why:'Rodents are about 4 in every 10 mammal species — from the pet hamster to the beaver — and this card explains the whole clan with one tooth.',
  dev:'Family-scale classification (biggest mammal family), cause-and-effect (chewing wears teeth down), and engineering play.',
  vocab:'rodent, gnaw, squirrel, porcupine, quills, chinchilla, dust bath, prairie dog, tunnels, colony',
  teach:'1) The tooth rule: rodent front teeth never stop growing — so they never stop chewing. That is the whole family in one fact.\n2) The squirrel: the planter — buries thousands of acorns, remembers most, and the forgotten ones become oaks.\n3) The porcupine: the walker-in-armor — quills are hairs with tiny barbs; it cannot throw them, but touch teaches respect.\n4) The chinchilla: the softest — fur so dense water cannot reach skin; it cleans with a dust bath, rolling in fine volcanic ash.\n5) The prairie dog: the town-builder — tunnels with nurseries and lookout mounds; the sentries whistle one warning call per predator.',
  say:'"Rodents have teeth that never stop growing — so they gnaw and gnaw! The squirrel plants forests by accident, the porcupine wears thirty thousand quills, the chinchilla bathes in dust, and the prairie dog runs a whole underground town with sentries on watch!"',
  notice:'On the card, the squirrel holds its acorn, the porcupine shows its quill coat, the chinchilla sits soft and round, and the prairie dog stands at its burrow mouth. Which one is a famous pet cousin? (the chinchilla — and the hamster next door)',
  ask:'"Why do rodents chew all day?" (their teeth never stop growing) "How does a chinchilla clean its fur?" (dust baths — water would never dry) "What does a prairie dog whistle?" (a warning — one call per predator)',
  childSay:'Your child might gnaw-teethe everything and narrate "rodent tooth care", demand a dust-bath station for the stuffed chinchilla, or pop up from cushion tunnels whistling predator warnings.',
  real:'Prairie dog towns dot the US plains — the sentries&rsquo; warning jumps are visible from the road. Chinchilla dust-bath videos are the softest thing on the internet.',
  play:'Tunnel town: couch-cushion tunnels with one lookout popping up to whistle. Then the hay relay: carry "grass" (paper strips) to the winter pile against a timer, exactly like the pika&rsquo;s cousin work.',
  check:'Ask: "What never stops growing on a rodent?" (its front teeth) "Why dust baths?" (fur too dense for water) "What is a prairie dog town?" (tunnel colonies with lookout sentries)'},
 'african-grassland-animals':{
  title:'The African Grassland Lesson',one:'The great grass stage.',
  seoTitle:'African Grassland Animals Lesson for Kids — Lion, Cheetah, Wildebeest and Ostrich, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: African grassland animals — lion, cheetah, wildebeest and ostrich. The sprint that lasts twenty seconds, the million-strong migration and the grassland relay.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the savanna cast, one stripe at a time.',
  what:'The African savanna is the great grass stage — warm, wide and full of runners: the lion, the cheetah, the wildebeest and the ostrich.',
  know:'Lions rest up to twenty hours a day and hunt in prides; a cheetah&rsquo;s sprint lasts about twenty seconds and then it must rest; wildebeest migrate in herds over a million strong; the ostrich is the biggest bird alive, runs at highway speed, and lays the biggest egg on Earth.',
  mean:'The stage has roles: the resting king, the twenty-second sprinter, the walking million and the feathered runner — grass powers them all.',
  why:'The savanna is the child&rsquo;s picture-book Africa — and this card fills it with real mechanics: even speed has a fuel budget.',
  dev:'Energy thinking (sprints cost everything), migration-scale wonder, and the biggest-egg math.',
  vocab:'savanna, grassland, lion, pride, cheetah, sprint, wildebeest, migration, ostrich, herd',
  teach:'1) The lion: the napper — most of the day in the shade; the pride&rsquo;s lionesses do much of the hunting, and the roar carries eight kilometers.\n2) The cheetah: the twenty-second champion — the fastest sprint on Earth, then a long rest; the tail is a rudder at full speed.\n3) The wildebeest: the walker — a million-strong migration chasing the rain across the plains, crossing crocodile rivers on the way.\n4) The ostrich: the feathered sprinter — runs at highway speed on two toes, and its eye is bigger than its brain.\n5) The grass idea: every role runs on grass — the wildebeest eats it, the hunters follow the wildebeest, the whole stage is powered by rain.',
  say:'"The African savanna is the great grass stage! The lion naps like a king and roars across eight kilometers, the cheetah sprints for twenty seconds flat, the wildebeest walks by the million, and the ostrich runs faster than the highway!"',
  notice:'On the card, the lion rests with its mane, the cheetah stands lean with tear-stripes on its face, the wildebeest hunches over its horns, and the ostrich towers on its running legs. Which one hunts in a team?',
  ask:'"How long can the cheetah sprint?" (about twenty seconds — then a long rest) "How many wildebeest migrate together?" (over a million) "Which bird runs instead of flying?" (the ostrich)',
  childSay:'Your child might sprint the yard and collapse dramatically "twenty seconds, exactly", nap like a lion with royal snoring, or measure an ostrich egg against every egg in the fridge.',
  real:'The wildebeest migration crosses Kenya and Tanzania yearly — river crossings are nature&rsquo;s biggest drama. Cheetah runs at wildlife parks show the twenty-second rule live.',
  play:'Grassland relay: the cheetah sprint (twenty counted seconds, all-out), the wildebeest walk (the family in one long line), the ostrich stride (biggest steps possible) — and the lion nap at the finish, royalty-resting.',
  check:'Ask: "Who hunts in a pride?" (the lion) "What chases the wildebeest migration?" (the rain — and the hunters who follow) "What is the biggest bird?" (the ostrich)'},
 'south-american-animals':{
  title:'The South American Animals Lesson',one:'The Andes to the Amazon.',
  seoTitle:'South American Animals Lesson for Kids — Llama, Sloth, Jaguar and Capybara, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: South American animals — llama, sloth, jaguar and capybara. The strongest bite in the big cats, the world&rsquo;s calmest rodent and the Andes-to-Amazon journey.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches South America&rsquo;s cast, one habitat at a time.',
  what:'South America stacks its habitats: high Andes, cloud forest, Amazon jungle — and this card climbs it: the llama up high, the sloth in the canopy, the jaguar below, the capybara at the river.',
  know:'Llamas carry loads on mountain trails and hum to each other; sloths descend their tree about once a week; jaguars have the strongest bite of any big cat and love to swim; capybaras are the world&rsquo;s biggest rodents and famously get along with everyone.',
  mean:'Height is the map: each animal owns its floor of the continent — mountain, canopy, jungle floor, riverbank.',
  why:'The capybara is the internet&rsquo;s favorite animal for a reason — calm friendship as a survival strategy is the lesson hiding in the meme.',
  dev:'Habitat-layering (a continent with floors), bite-force science, and the friendship strategy as social learning.',
  vocab:'South America, Andes, llama, hum, sloth, canopy, jaguar, bite, capybara, calm',
  teach:'1) The llama: the mountaineer — bred from wild guanacos to carry loads on Andes trails, humming the whole way up.\n2) The sloth: the slow resident — hangs in the canopy, descends about once a week (the toilet trip), and everything about it is slow on purpose.\n3) The jaguar: the strongest bite — it cracks turtle shells and caiman armor, and unlike most cats it swims for fun and dinner.\n4) The capybara: the giant guinea pig — dog-sized, grass-eating, and so calm that birds ride its head; whole ponds relax around it.\n5) The floor plan: mountain → canopy → jungle → river: one continent, four addresses, one card.',
  say:'"South America stacks its homes! The llama hums up the mountain trails, the sloth hangs in the canopy moving in slow motion, the jaguar has the strongest bite and swims, and the capybara — a giant guinea pig — gets along with absolutely everyone!"',
  notice:'On the card, the llama stands high and woolly, the sloth hangs from its branch, the jaguar crouches with its rosette coat, and the capybara sits calm as a stone. Which one would you meet highest up the mountain?',
  ask:'"Where does the llama work?" (carrying loads on Andes mountain trails) "What is the jaguar famous for?" (the strongest bite of any big cat — and swimming) "How big is a capybara?" (the world&rsquo;s biggest rodent — dog-sized)',
  childSay:'Your child might hum up every staircase "llama freight", slow-motion everything for one sloth morning, or invite every toy in the house into the capybara bath.',
  real:'Llama treks are real in the Peruvian Andes; jaguar conservation reserves run through the Pantanal wetlands of Brazil. The capybara hot-spring photos from Japanese zoos are the card&rsquo;s official mascot image.',
  play:'Capybara hot spring: warm basin, every toy invited, calm faces mandatory. Then the llama freight walk: backpack on, cushion trail, no drops. The sloth finishes it — one deliberate toe-to-heel lap, slowest wins.',
  check:'Ask: "Which animal hums?" (the llama) "What can the jaguar crack with its bite?" (turtle shells — the strongest bite in the big cats) "What is the capybara&rsquo;s superpower?" (calm — everyone gets along with it)'},
 'arctic-animals-2':{
  title:'The More Arctic Animals Lesson',one:'Second visit to the ice.',
  seoTitle:'More Arctic Animals Lesson for Kids — Arctic Hare, Snowy Owl, Musk Ox and Arctic Tern, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: more arctic animals — arctic hare, snowy owl, musk ox and arctic tern. The 70,000-kilometer migration and the coat-change game.',
  intro:'This lesson goes back to the ice for the specialists, one migration at a time.',
  what:'The Arctic deserves a second card: the arctic hare, the snowy owl, the musk ox and the arctic tern are the deep-winter specialists — the animals that stay where others only visit.',
  know:'The arctic hare sprints at 60 km/h over snow; the snowy owl hunts even when the sun never sets; musk oxen huddle in a defensive circle; the arctic tern flies pole to pole every year — about 70,000 kilometers, the longest migration of any animal.',
  mean:'Staying is the strategy: where the polar bear card showed the hunters, this card shows the residents — animals built to live the whole year in the cold.',
  why:'The arctic tern is the animal kingdom&rsquo;s endurance record — and it is smaller than a pigeon, which rewrites every "too small" a child has ever heard.',
  dev:'Migration as geography (pole to pole), seasonal coat-change, and the huddle as teamwork science.',
  vocab:'arctic, hare, snowy owl, musk ox, arctic tern, migration, pole to pole, huddle, coat, survive',
  teach:'1) The arctic hare: the sprinter — white in winter, brown in summer, and fast enough to outrun a fox across open snow.\n2) The snowy owl: the day hunter — in the summer the sun never sets, so this owl hunts in daylight, feathers to the toes.\n3) The musk ox: the circle — when danger comes, the herd forms a ring, horns out, calves safe inside; teamwork older than any town wall.\n4) The arctic tern: the champion — flies from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back every year: two summers, no winter, 70,000 kilometers.\n5) The map: put a finger on the top of the world and drag it to the bottom — the tern does that twice a year.',
  say:'"The Arctic has residents, not just visitors! The arctic hare sprints across the snow, the snowy owl hunts in the midnight sun, the musk oxen stand in a circle of horns, and the arctic tern flies pole to pole — seventy thousand kilometers, every year!"',
  notice:'On the card, the arctic hare sits low and white, the snowy owl stares its round stare, the musk ox shags like a haystack with horns, and the arctic tern points its streamer wings. Which one travels the farthest?',
  ask:'"How far does the arctic tern fly each year?" (about 70,000 kilometers — pole to pole and back) "What do musk oxen do when danger comes?" (form a circle, horns out) "What changes on the arctic hare between seasons?" (its coat — white winter, brown summer)',
  childSay:'Your child might drag a finger across every map from pole to pole "tern style", circle-up the whole family back-to-back "musk ox formation", or refuse all winter coats as "my fur grows in".',
  real:'Arctic terns nest in Iceland, Canada and Alaska — the streamer-tailed flyers are visible all summer. Musk oxen live in northern Canada and Greenland; their qiviut wool is softer than cashmere.',
  play:'Coat-change game: one end of the room is winter (white scarf), one is summer (brown) — run and change as the caller calls. Then the tern flight: cross the room twice without touching down, and the musk-ox circle at the end.',
  check:'Ask: "Which bird makes the longest migration on Earth?" (the arctic tern) "What is the musk oxen&rsquo;s defense?" (the circle — horns out, calves inside) "Why can the snowy owl hunt in summer daylight?" (the arctic sun never sets)'},
 'cave-animals':{
  title:'The Cave Animals Lesson',one:'Life without any sunlight.',
  seoTitle:'Cave Animals Lesson for Kids — Cave Bat, Salamander, Cave Cricket and Olm, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: cave animals — cave bat, salamander, cave cricket and olm. The hundred-year blind olm, echo navigation and the echo-point game.',
  intro:'This lesson goes underground, one echo at a time.',
  what:'Caves are the darkest homes on Earth — no sunlight, ever — and their animals have traded eyes for other tools: the cave bat, the pale salamander, the cave cricket and the legendary olm.',
  know:'Bats navigate by echolocation — sound pictures; cave salamanders and crickets grow pale without needing color; cave crickets leave at night to forage and carry food home; the olm is a blind, white, cave-only amphibian that can live a hundred years.',
  mean:'Trade-offs are the idea: in a world with no light, eyes are expensive and useless — so evolution trades them for ears, whiskers and patience.',
  why:'The olm is the cave&rsquo;s living legend: blind, pearl-white, nearly motionless, and older than the countries it lives in — the deepest "different is normal" lesson on the deck.',
  dev:'Sensory substitution (sound as sight), patience-science, and comfort in the dark through game structure.',
  vocab:'cave, dark, bat, echo, salamander, pale, cave cricket, olm, blind, hundred years',
  teach:'1) The light rule: caves have NO sun — no day, no season, just cool dark. Everything on this card has made peace with that.\n2) The cave bat: the echo-eyes — it shouts tiny shouts and maps the cave from the bounce-back, flying fast in absolute dark.\n3) The salamander: the pale one — cave salamanders lose their color; in the dark, nobody can see it anyway.\n4) The cave cricket: the shopper — it leaves the cave at night to find food and hops back home with full pockets.\n5) The olm: the legend — blind, white as a pearl, barely moves, and lives up to a century in the same cold pool. Scientists tracked some sitting still for seven years.',
  say:'"Caves have no sunlight — ever! The bat flies by echo — shout, listen, map! The salamander turned pale, the cricket shops outside and hops home, and the olm is blind and white and can live a hundred years in one pool. The dark is home here!"',
  notice:'On the card, the cave bat spreads its wings, the salamander curves pale and smooth, the cricket stands on long legs, and the olm floats like a ghost with tiny legs. Which one has NO eyes worth using?',
  ask:'"How does the bat see in the dark?" (echoes — sound pictures) "Why is the cave salamander pale?" (no light — color is wasted) "What is special about the olm?" (blind, white, and it can live a hundred years)',
  childSay:'Your child might echo-point across dark rooms with eyes closed, build a "cave" of blankets and stay cheerfully blind for an afternoon, or draw the olm&rsquo;s portrait as "a ghost with toes".',
  real:'Real olms live in the caves of Slovenia and Croatia — the "human fish", locals call it. Bat-cave tours in every country end with the echo demonstration: one shout, a thousand answers.',
  play:'Echo location: one person closes their eyes and calls "where am I?" — the family answers from new spots and the listener points. Then the blanket cave: sit inside, listen to every sound, and name it without looking.',
  check:'Ask: "What light is in a cave?" (none — ever) "What does the bat use instead of eyes?" (echoes) "How long can an olm live?" (up to a hundred years)'},
 'mountain-animals-1':{
  title:'The Mountain Animals Lesson',one:'Climbers of the thin air.',
  seoTitle:'Mountain Animals Lesson for Kids — Mountain Goat, Snow Leopard, Yak and Marmot, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: mountain animals — mountain goat, snow leopard, yak and marmot. Rubber hooves, the balance tail and the marmot whistle, with a balance-beam trail.',
  intro:'This lesson climbs to the high cliffs, one ledge at a time.',
  what:'High mountains have thin air, cold wind and steep stone — and their animals are built for all three: the mountain goat, the snow leopard, the yak and the marmot.',
  know:'Mountain goats have rubbery pads built into their hooves and walk ledges people need ropes for; the snow leopard&rsquo;s tail is as long as its body — balance beam and scarf in one; yaks carry loads through snow at altitudes that leave climbers gasping; marmots whistle predator warnings and sleep up to eight months.',
  mean:'Grip and warmth are the mountain game: every animal on this card has solved the same two problems — not falling, not freezing.',
  why:'The mountain is the deck&rsquo;s most extreme engineering exam, and the snow leopard&rsquo;s tail is the neatest answer on any card.',
  dev:'Balance as body-science, adaptation matching (hoof, tail, coat, whistle), and altitude respect.',
  vocab:'mountain, cliff, mountain goat, hooves, snow leopard, balance, yak, marmot, whistle, altitude',
  teach:'1) The mountain goat: the climber — rubbery hoof-pads grip stone like climbing shoes; kids at ledges people rope up for.\n2) The snow leopard: the balancer — the long tail swings like a tightrope-walker&rsquo;s pole, then wraps around the face as a scarf at night.\n3) The yak: the freighter — huge lungs, a shaggy coat, and the calm to carry loads through snow at heights that stop mountaineers.\n4) The marmot: the whistler — one sharp tweet means "eagle!" to the whole colony; everyone dives for the burrow.\n5) The mountain rule: up high, the air is thin — the yak&rsquo;s big lungs and the climbers&rsquo; slow steps answer the same problem.',
  say:'"Mountain animals climb where the air is thin and the rocks are steep! The mountain goat walks cliffs on rubbery hooves, the snow leopard balances with its giant tail, the yak carries loads through the snow, and the marmot whistles — tweet! — eagle alert!"',
  notice:'On the card, the mountain goat stands on a ledge, the snow leopard curls its long tail, the yak hunches under its shaggy coat, and the marmot sits upright at its burrow. Which one uses its tail for balance AND warmth?',
  ask:'"What is built into a mountain goat&rsquo;s hooves?" (rubbery pads — nature&rsquo;s climbing shoes) "What does the snow leopard do with its tail?" (balances like a pole, then wraps it as a scarf) "What does the marmot&rsquo;s whistle mean?" (danger — usually an eagle)',
  childSay:'Your child might walk every curb "goat ledge" with arms out, wrap a scarf-tail for snow-leopard naps, or whistle-alert the whole house before sliding into a cushion burrow.',
  real:'Mountain goats are visible from trails across the Canadian and US Rockies — look up, not just out. Snow leopards live wild across Central Asia; marmots whistle in every high meadow.',
  play:'Balance-beam mountain: a tape line on the floor, arms up like a leopard tail — forward, backward, eyes-forward-only. Then the whistle drill: two tweets means the cushion burrow, everybody dives.',
  check:'Ask: "Why does the mountain goat never fall?" (rubbery hoof-pads grip the stone) "What are the snow leopard&rsquo;s two tail jobs?" (balance and scarf) "How long do marmots sleep in winter?" (up to eight months)'},
 'mountain-animals-2':{
  title:'The More Mountain Animals Lesson',one:'The high life, second ascent.',
  seoTitle:'More Mountain Animals Lesson for Kids — Ibex, Chamois, Pika and Condor, Step by Step (Nature Club Animals)',
  metaDescription:'A simple Nature Club spotter lesson for ages 4–5: more mountain animals — ibex, chamois, pika and condor. The haystack winter, the no-flap condor flight and the counting-the-rings horn fact.',
  intro:'This lesson takes the second ascent, one horn-ring at a time.',
  what:'The mountains get a second card because the high life is crowded: the ibex with its curved horns, the leaping chamois, the hay-stacking pika and the giant condor.',
  know:'An ibex&rsquo;s horns grow one ring per year — you can count its age; chamois bounce between rock ledges on elastic legs; pikas do not hibernate — they cut and dry grass into haystacks all summer; condors ride the wind for hours on wings wider than a car, barely flapping.',
  mean:'Preparation is the idea: the mountain winter is coming for everyone — the pika stores hay, the ibex stores horn-rings, the condor stores altitude.',
  why:'The pika is the card&rsquo;s tiny hero: no hibernation, no migration — just relentless summer work, and it works.',
  dev:'Age-counting science (horn rings like tree rings), preparation-vs-hibernation strategies, and gliding physics.',
  vocab:'ibex, horns, rings, chamois, leap, pika, haystack, condor, glide, wingspan',
  teach:'1) The ibex: the horn-counter — big curved horns with a ring per year of life; cliff-walkers on hooves as sure as the goat&rsquo;s.\n2) The chamois: the bouncer — elastic legs leap rock to rock where falling is not an option; antelope of the high peaks.\n3) The pika: the farmer — a tiny rabbit-cousin that spends summer cutting grass and drying it into haystacks for winter — no sleep, just storage.\n4) The condor: the glider — wings wider than a car ride the mountain wind for hours; flapping is for takeoff and emergencies only.\n5) The winter question: how do YOU store for a hard season? The pika&rsquo;s haystack is the card&rsquo;s answer.',
  say:'"The mountains get a second crew! The ibex has horns with a ring for every year, the chamois bounces rock to rock, the pika stacks haystacks for winter — no sleeping in! — and the condor flies on wings wider than a car without flapping once!"',
  notice:'On the card, the ibex shows the big curved horns, the chamois crouches springy, the pika sits tiny beside its hay, and the condor spreads the whole square. Which one is the smallest — and the hardest working?',
  ask:'"How can you tell an ibex&rsquo;s age?" (count the rings on its horns) "What does the pika do all summer?" (cuts and dries grass into haystacks) "How far can a condor fly without flapping?" (hours — riding the wind)',
  childSay:'Your child might count ring-drawings on every horn "ibex birthday", stack a hay-pile of everything in the art drawer, or glide the hallway with strict no-flap rules.',
  real:'Ibex herds live in the Alps and can be watched from cable cars; Andean condors soar over Patagonia — the two-and-a-half-meter wingspan is real. Pikas squeak from talus fields all over the western mountains.',
  play:'Haystack relay: collect "grass" (paper strips) from one wall to the winter pile at the other — against a timer, pika rules. Then condor glide: wall to wall, arms wide, NO flapping — touchdown counts.',
  check:'Ask: "What does a horn ring mean?" (one year of life) "Why does the pika stack hay?" (no hibernation — winter food stored in advance) "What keeps a condor up without flapping?" (riding the mountain wind)'}
};

